  "worked_solution": "The function is increasing where ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$ \n\nPreviously, we found that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x)=-6{x}^{2}+12x+18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to solve the inequality\n\n$$\n-6{x}^{2}+12x+18>0.\n$$\n\nThe above inequality factors as\n\n$$\n-6(x+1)(x-3)>0.\n$$\n\nThe graph of $y=-6(x+1)(x-3)$ has roots at $x=-1$ and $x=3,$ as shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14285419/assets/55991a532089f011a51f325a7872d4a8f6d7a3f65297e61772d5472582b8a255.png)\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the function is increasing for $x\\in (-1,3).$",
